e are $2\\times 1=2$ shaded pieces in total. Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{7}\\times 2=\\frac{2}{7}.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186510391 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186510275 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186510391 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186510310 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186510391 #edb/ref 1069 "q-192943" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186510391 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186510391 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/3c/3c343c4aa57c4b700a380ac2e44b616c822b13fd6fc93ebb233bf30c30069585.png)\n\nThe multiplication problem represented by the model above is\n\n{{field-1}}\n$\\times \\,\\,3=$\n{{field-2}}." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186510391 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "4ffb5f5e-d005-5651-a067-5254cbe8580f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186510391 #edb/ref 1074 "We have $\\frac{3}{20}$ on the left and $\\frac{9}{20}$ on the right.\n\n![](images/fd/fd68488765db4be3c6dd48dc19658bcc73fd035d1b0ed669bf910c502015b05a.png)\n\nTherefore, the multiplication problem shown is\n\n$$\n\\frac{3}{20}\\times 3=\\frac{9}{20}.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186510394 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186510330 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186510394 #edb/ref 1069 "q-91803" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186510394 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186510394 #edb/ref 1071 "$5÷\\frac{1}{14}=$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186510394 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "594339a7-9976-5ba0-b28f-d33a28c1ef05" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186510394 #edb/ref 1074 "To divide a whole number by a unit fraction, we multiply the whole number by the fraction's denominator:\n\n$$\n5÷\\frac{1}{14}=5\\times 14=70\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186510413 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186510313 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186510413 #edb/ref 1069 "q-5747" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186510413 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186510413 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/6e/6e4cf483b68db33f213470f247ee118cad452c269e8420431730f457f5b5ca89.png)\n\nWhat are the $xy$-coordinates of the point shown in the axes above?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186510413 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "7a1c9a9e-192b-564f-9f04-0763b9d472b4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186510413 #edb/ref 1074 "To find the coordinates of the point, we inspect its position along the $x$ and $y$ axes.\n\nThe point is $4$ units to the *left* of the origin, in the *negative* direction of the $x$-axis. So, the point has an $x$-coordinate of $-4.$\n\nAdditionally, the point is $8$ units *below* the origin, in the *negative* direction of the $y$-axis. So, the point has a $y$-coordinate of $-8.$\n\nThe point has an $x$-coordinate of $-4$ and a $y$-coordinate of $-8,$ so its coordinates are $(-4,-8).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186510415 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186510274 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186510415 #edb/ref 1069 "q-137575" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186510415 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186510415 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/9f/9fccca73fa8a954f7bfe451021ef4331d1775211a15203ae511657243fbf61e5.png)\n\nWhat picture is missing from the multiplication model above?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186510415 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "7d6b5a99-b4f7-505b-8d4c-543bc5253331" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186510415 #edb/ref 1074 "In the model, we have $\\frac{2}{4}$ on the left.\n\nSince we need to multiply by $\\frac{2}{3}$, we split the whole into $3$ equal parts horizontally and ensure that $2$ of the parts remain shaded.\n\n![](images/53/53ca2220480be7f22b42f8e18df615295868408d7e2b7858c5dd2b17656b1ba8.png)\n\nFrom the picture on the right, we obtain that the shaded part represents $\\frac{4}{12}$ of the whole.\n\nTherefore, the missing picture is as follows:\n\n![](images/8e/8e4d072f17abd83c86c3c2ba9ea3fec409a01666ec1f96e6b5c16a7e744d597e.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186510420 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186510326 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186510420 #edb/ref 1069 "q-61588" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186510420 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186510420 #edb/ref 1071 "What fraction is missing from the statement below?\n\n$$\n16\\times \\frac{5}{8}=\\frac{\\bbox[2pt,border:1pt solid black]{\\phantom{A}}}{\\bbox[2pt,border:1pt solid black]{\\phantom{A}}}\\times 5\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186510420 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "852fda9f-31ba-5fd4-8170-a556e23b68b9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186510420 #edb/ref 1074 "Interpreting the fraction as division, we can write our expression as:\n\n$$\n16\\times 5÷8\n$$\n\nWe can write an equivalent expression by swapping the $\\times 5$ and the $÷8:$\n\n$$\n16÷8\\times 5\n$$\n\nFinally, expressing the division as a fraction, we arrive at:\n\n$$\n\\frac{16}{8}\\times 5\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186510435 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186510281 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186510435 #edb/ref 1069 "q-138165" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186510435 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186510435 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/9e/9e10a780a01897be9acca1003a02451c33fb14d6e111deb226f16fb581735a51.png)\n\nWhat picture is missing from the division model above?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186510435 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "a3e34c03-c5d1-5308-b87f-8c0345829c50" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186510435 #edb/ref 1074 "In the model, we have $\\frac{1}{4}$ on the left.\n\nSince we need to divide by $4$, we split the whole into $4$ equal parts horizontally.\n\n![](images/3e/3e28688c20e4784b50687b0a36e08f59d828b03cf357365ceac507667b0d0d5d.png)\n\nFrom the picture on the right, we obtain that the shaded part represents $\\frac{1}{16}$ of the whole.\n\nThe missing picture is:\n\n![](images/bc/bcb343c11b184e57229b033d07817655c0cd0fb926a419afbf84e1d115a086ec.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186510438 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186510353 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186510438 #edb/ref 1069 "q-75559" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186510438 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186510438 #edb/ref 1071 "Translate the following phrase into an algebraic expression: \"Twelve more than a number.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186510438 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "a89a0beb-9081-5694-a8c0-2b633fb2ad70" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186510438 #edb/ref 1074 "If we denote our number by $x$, then the phrase \"twelve more than a number\" can be written as\n\n$$\nx+12.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186510444 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186510250 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186510444 #edb/ref 1069 "q-91969" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186510444 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186510444 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/c7/c7e2d2b4be3fbfe1cb0944dec21c0779fc197d1282eef5a56e806f9ce56a4962.png)\n\nWhat picture is missing from the multiplication model above?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186510444 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "b577ef62-3bc6-5dd8-a240-3b3950135891" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186510444 #edb/ref 1074 "In the model, we have $\\frac{2}{5}$ on the left.\n\nSince we need to multiply by $\\frac{1}{3}$, we split the whole into $3$ equal parts horizontally.\n\n![](images/4e/4e6aa019748eccb9faeb9cb87632e4545d74f4960bdfab3b7a50aa4065069c48.png)\n\nFrom the picture on the right, we obtain that the shaded part represents $\\frac{2}{15}$ of the whole.\n\nThe missing picture is:\n\n![](images/32/32e9dd52864c9183df7b617cb60152e57d8b2635ea46e639d82d029288faa269.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186510455 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186510268 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186510455 #edb/ref 1069 "q-59838" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186510455 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186510455 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/23/23196761364659fb01eb42ffd37c898331a654bf52e3ec1ebe79316f986a6d7e.png)\n\nEach of the two shapes in the model above represents one whole. From left to right, what numbers are missing from the division problem below?\n\n$$\n\\boxed{\\,\\,}÷\\frac{1}{3}=\\boxed{\\,\\,}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186510455 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "cf8a9fa4-0c6b-5d86-8b4f-17e214ee6603" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186510455 #edb/ref 1074 "In the model, there are $2$ shapes. Each shape consists of $3$ pieces that each represent $\\frac{1}{3}$ of a whole. There are $6$ pieces in total.\n\nThe model tells us that when we divide $2$ wholes into thirds, we will be left with $6$ pieces.\n\nHence, the division problem is:\n\n$$\n\\boxed{\\,2\\,}÷\\frac{1}{3}=\\boxed{\\,6\\,}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the missing numbers are $2$ and $6.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186510460 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186510299 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186510460 #edb/ref 1069 "q-58378" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186510460 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186510460 #edb/ref 1071 "$\\frac{1}{12}÷3=$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186510460 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "da75b2dd-3237-5ce6-9291-2b2e45f7ef20" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186510460 #edb/ref 1074 "To divide a unit fraction by a whole number, we multiply the fraction's denominator by that whole number:\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{12}÷3=\\frac{1}{12\\times 3}=\\frac{1}{36}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186510480 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186510528 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186510480 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "0d938664-32ae-58c2-9276-34c95d3f3781" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186510480 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186510390 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186510482 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186510485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186510482 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "11f026d1-05fd-58c9-977e-71070693efa3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186510482 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186510420 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186510485 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186510521 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186510485 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "19655f53-67a7-53d6-ba70-32929dea4065" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [1759218651048